title: Point Properties
keyword: POINT
manual: graphics/#c2-point

Calculates the value, gradient, Hessian and other properties of a field of the system at a
point, and reports them in a table. Use it to probe a density, an orbital or any other field
at an atom, at a critical point or at an arbitrary position. The point is marked by a small
sphere in the view while the window is open, and the report is recalculated whenever the
point, the field or the list of properties changes.

# Options

- "Field": the field whose properties are calculated at the point.
- "Point": the coordinates of the point, fractional in a crystal and in Å in a molecule.
  "Pick" lets you click the point in the view: an atom, a critical point or a point in empty
  space.
- "Point properties of the system": what is calculated. The built-in properties of the field
  (value, gradient, Hessian, ...) come first, then the point properties defined for the
  system and, if the system has more than one field, the values of all the other fields. For
  each one, "Show" decides whether it appears in the table, "Format" chooses fixed or
  exponential notation and "Decimals" the number of decimal places. The delete icon removes a
  point property from the system.
- "New": adds a point property, as the POINTPROP keyword does. Choose an arithmetic expression
  of the fields (give it a "Name" of at most 10 characters and the "Expression", for instance
  "$1 - $2") or one of the named functions, calculated from the field of the window, then
  press "Add".

# Results

The "Properties" section shows the table: the coordinates of the point (fractional in
crystals, bohr and Å), followed by one row per property. Right-click the table header to
copy the table or write it to a file. Error messages appear below the table.

# Notes

The point properties belong to the system: a property added here is also available in the
Critical Points window, and vice versa.
